<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">ExxonMobil</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1999">1999</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Exxon Mobil Corporation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">ExxonMobil is an American oil and gas company formed in 1999 by the merger of Exxon and Mobil, both descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-d-rockefeller">John D. Rockefeller</a>'s Standard Oil. One of the world's largest companies, it discovered the large Liza oil field off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyana">Guyana</a> in 2015 and began production there in 2019, transforming Guyana's economy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/exxonmobil.jpg" alt="ExxonMobil" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lanzao0o1 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cube_xom_mine.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>ExxonMobil is the largest direct successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-d-rockefeller">John D. Rockefeller</a>'s Standard Oil. One of its two roots, the Vacuum Oil Company, was founded in 1866, acquired by Standard Oil in 1879 and divested in 1911 when Standard Oil was broken up under a legal order. In 1931 Vacuum Oil merged with the Standard Oil Company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> (Socony), and the combined company later became known as Mobil.</p><p>The other root was Standard Oil's New Jersey subsidiary, called Jersey Standard, which after the breakup kept the Standard Oil name across much of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It grew by acquiring Humble Oil in the 1930s and became the dominant oil company on the world stage. Because it did not own the Standard Oil name throughout the country, it sold its products under three brands, Esso, Enco and Humble Oil, and in 1972 it chose the single name Exxon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The two companies agreed in 1998 to merge, and the deal closed on 30 November 1999. The new name joined the trade names of both predecessors. In legal terms Exxon was the surviving company and bought Mobil, rather than a new company being created, and Exxon's NYSE ticker symbol changed from XON to XOM. ExxonMobil Chemical was created in 1999 by joining the chemical businesses of both firms.</p><p>In 1989 an Exxon tanker discharged about 11 million US gallons (42,000 m3) of oil into Prince William Sound in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a>, oiling 1,300 miles (2,100 km) of remote coastline. The Exxon Valdez spill remains one of the largest in American history. In the late 1980s Mobil hired units of the Indonesian army to guard its gas project in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a>, and the units were accused of human rights violations. The company left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> in 2001 and settled the resulting lawsuit in 2023, without admitting wrongdoing. In 2022 the company combined its separate downstream and chemical divisions into a Product Solutions division and formed a Low Carbon Solutions division.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>ExxonMobil is vertically integrated across the oil and gas industry, from exploration and extraction to refining and retail, and has a large chemicals business producing plastics and synthetic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>. It markets fuel under the brands Exxon, Esso and Mobil. Exxon is used only in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, while Esso and Mobil are used in most other countries. Since 2008 Mobil has been the only brand for the company's lubricants. Its headquarters are at Spring, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, a suburb of Houston, where the company moved from Irving.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “ExxonMobil” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In September 1994 the State Oil Company of Azerbaijan Republic (SOCAR) signed a 30-year contract with 13 oil companies, among them Amoco, BP, <b>ExxonMobil</b>, Lukoil and Equinor, opening deepwater fields untouched by Soviet exploitation; the State Oil Fund was set up to hold the revenue for future generations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>ExxonMobil</b> discovered large offshore oil fields in 2015, and production began in 2019, making Guyana one of the world's fastest-growing economies.</blockquote><a href="/state/co-operative-republic-of-guyana">Co-operative Republic of Guyana</a></li></ul></section>
